---
import BaseLayout from "theme/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { siteUrl } from "theme/i18n/config";

const lang = "en";
const dir = "ltr";
const slug = "handling-time-zones-javascript";
const seoTitle = "Handling Time Zones in JavaScript: Complete Developer Guide (2026)";
const pageDescription = "Master handling time zones in JavaScript. Learn native Date, Intl.DateTimeFormat, ECMAScript Temporal API, Luxon, Day.js, and server-side rendering UTC rules.";

const pubDate = "2026-07-30T14:00:00+05:00";
const updatedDate = "2026-07-30T14:00:00+05:00";

const meta = {
  seoTitle,
  description: pageDescription,
  keywords: "handling time zones javascript, js date time zone, intl datetimeformat, temporal api javascript, luxon vs dayjs, momentjs deprecation, utc date javascript, nodejs timezone",
  image: "/assets/js-timezone-guide.svg",
  imageAlt: "JavaScript Time Zone Architecture Diagram",
  pubDate,
  updatedDate,
  author: "Jackie Bruce",
  faqs: [
    {
      q: "Does a JavaScript Date object store time zone information inside it?",
      a: "No. A JavaScript `Date` object internally stores a single numerical value: the number of milliseconds elapsed since January 1, 1970 00:00:00 UTC (the Unix Epoch). It has no internal property recording a specific time zone name. It only formats that timestamp using the host environment's local time zone or UTC."
    },
    {
      q: "Why is Moment.js deprecated and what should I use instead?",
      a: "Moment.js was declared legacy by its maintainers because it is mutable (leading to bugs), expensive in bundle size (tree-shaking impossible), and relies on outdated OOP paradigms. Modern alternatives include native `Intl.DateTimeFormat`, the ECMAScript `Temporal` API, Luxon, Day.js, or date-fns-tz."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I format a date string in a specific time zone using native JS?",
      a: "Use `Intl.DateTimeFormat` with the `timeZone` option specified, e.g., `new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', { timeZone: 'America/New_York' }).format(new Date())`."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the ECMAScript Temporal API?",
      a: "The `Temporal` API is the modern native JavaScript replacement for `Date`. It introduces immutable objects like `Temporal.ZonedDateTime`, `Temporal.Instant`, and `Temporal.PlainDateTime`, providing first-class time zone support natively without external libraries."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I avoid hydration mismatch errors with time zones in Next.js or Astro?",
      a: "Hydration errors occur when the server renders HTML using UTC (or server local time) while the browser client renders using local client time. Fix this by delaying time zone formatting to client-side lifecycle hooks (`useEffect` or Astro client scripts) or enforcing a fixed time zone baseline across SSR."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I convert a local date string to a UTC ISO string in JavaScript?",
      a: "Use `new Date(dateString).toISOString()`, which parses the date string and returns a standardized ISO 8601 string formatted in UTC (ending in 'Z')."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I change the time zone of Node.js process at runtime?",
      a: "You can set the `TZ` environment variable before running your application (`TZ=UTC node app.js`) or dynamically via `process.env.TZ = 'UTC'`. Note that changing `process.env.TZ` mid-execution can be unreliable across different C++ runtime layers."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the difference between ISO 8601 and RFC 3339 timestamp strings?",
      a: "Both format timestamps as `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss.sssZ`. RFC 3339 is a strict profile of ISO 8601 specifically designed for internet protocols. JavaScript's `.toISOString()` complies with both."
    },
    {
      q: "Why does `new Date('2026-07-30')` parse as UTC midnight, but `new Date('2026/07/30')` parses as local midnight?",
      a: "According to the ECMAScript spec, date-only ISO strings (`YYYY-MM-DD`) are parsed as UTC midnight, whereas non-standard strings or slash separators are parsed as local midnight, leading to infamous off-by-one day bugs."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I get a user's IANA time zone identifier in the browser?",
      a: "Call `Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone`. This returns a string like `'America/New_York'` or `'Europe/London'."
    },
    {
      q: "How do Luxon and Day.js handle IANA time zones?",
      a: "Luxon uses native `Intl` under the hood to calculate offsets without shipping external binary files. Day.js requires the `timezone` and `utc` plugins (`dayjs.extend(utc); dayjs.extend(timezone)`)."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I add 1 month to a date across Daylight Saving Time transitions in JavaScript?",
      a: "Adding 30 days in raw milliseconds (`30 * 86400 * 1000`) will fail across DST transitions because a day during transition has 23 or 25 hours. Use `Temporal.ZonedDateTime` or libraries like Luxon that adjust calendar months while preserving wall-clock times."
    },
    {
      q: "What is `getTimezoneOffset()` in JavaScript and why is its sign inverted?",
      a: "The `Date.prototype.getTimezoneOffset()` method returns the difference in minutes between UTC and local time. Crucially, the sign is inverted: New York (UTC-5) returns positive `300`, while Tokyo (UTC+9) returns negative `-540`."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I parse a date string with an explicit offset in JavaScript?",
      a: "Pass an ISO string containing an explicit offset offset, e.g., `new Date('2026-07-30T14:00:00-04:00')`. JavaScript will parse the exact instant accurately into UTC."
    },
    {
      q: "How does `date-fns-tz` differ from Luxon?",
      a: "`date-fns-tz` operates on plain JavaScript `Date` objects using pure functions (ideal for functional programming and tree-shaking), whereas Luxon uses immutable `DateTime` class wrapper instances."
    },
    {
      q: "Can I use `Intl.DateTimeFormat` to list all available time zones?",
      a: "Yes. In modern browsers and Node.js, `Intl.supportedValuesOf('timeZone')` returns an array of all supported IANA time zone strings."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I store dates in MongoDB or PostgreSQL using JavaScript backend?",
      a: "Always serialize dates to ISO 8601 UTC strings or pass native JavaScript `Date` objects to ORMs (Prisma, TypeORM, Mongoose), which convert them to UTC timestamp columns."
    },
    {
      q: "What is `date.toLocaleDateString()` and why shouldn't it be used for data interchange?",
      a: "`toLocaleDateString()` outputs locale-sensitive strings designed for end-user display (e.g. `'7/30/2026'`). Never parse or send these strings to APIs, as formats vary across user devices."
    },
    {
      q: "How do I schedule a recurrent cron job across DST shifts in Node.js?",
      a: "Use cron libraries that accept explicit IANA time zone options (such as `node-cron` with `{ timezone: 'America/New_York' }`) to ensure jobs execute at the expected local wall-clock hour."
    },
    {
      q: "Where can I test live time zone conversions between GMT and EST?",
      a: "Use our interactive <a href='/gmt-to-est/'>GMT to EST Converter</a> and developer tools."
    }
  ]
};

const pageUrlPath = `${siteUrl}/handling-time-zones-javascript/`;

const schemas = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "TechArticle",
    "@id": `${pageUrlPath}#article`,
    "mainEntityOfPage": pageUrlPath,
    "headline": seoTitle,
    "description": pageDescription,
    "datePublished": pubDate,
    "dateModified": updatedDate,
    "inLanguage": "en",
    "author": {
      "@type": "Person",
      "name": "Jackie Bruce",
      "jobTitle": "Senior Time Zone Analyst",
      "url": `${siteUrl}/author/jackie-bruce/`
    },
    "publisher": {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": "GMT to EST Converter",
      "logo": {
        "@type": "ImageObject",
        "url": `${siteUrl}/logo.svg`
      }
    }
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  lang={lang}
  dir={dir}
  title={seoTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  slug={slug}
  meta={meta}
  mode="gmt-est"
  enableFaqSchema={true}
  enableArticleSchema={true}
  allowAds={true}
  breadcrumbHomeLabel="Home"
  breadcrumbLabel="JavaScript Time Zones"
  hreflangCodes={["en"]}
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schemas)} slot="head" />

  <!-- HERO & SUMMARY SECTION -->
  <section class="hero-banner" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <h1 id="hero-title" class="hero-banner-title speakable-headline">
      Handling Time Zones in JavaScript: <span class="gradient-text">Complete Modern Developer Guide</span>
    </h1>
  </section>

  <!-- Editorial Info Bar -->
  <div class="editorial-meta-bar" aria-label="Editorial metadata">
    <span>Last Updated: <strong>July 30, 2026</strong></span>
    <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
    <span>Editorially Reviewed by <a href="/author/jackie-bruce/"><strong>Jackie Bruce</strong></a></span>
    <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
    <span>ECMAScript Standard Specification</span>
  </div>

  <section class="acdt-intro content-section" aria-label="Quick Answer Summary">
    <div class="summary-box info-callout speakable-summary">
      <h3>Quick Answer: How Should JavaScript Developers Handle Time Zones?</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Always store and transmit dates as UTC ISO 8601 strings (or Unix epoch milliseconds), and format them into local time zones only at the UI boundary.</strong> 
        The native legacy `Date` object internally stores raw UTC epoch milliseconds and lacks multi-timezone manipulation. Modern JavaScript development relies on `Intl.DateTimeFormat`, the ECMAScript `Temporal` API, or lightweight libraries like Luxon, Day.js, and date-fns-tz for robust time zone conversions across client and server environments.
      </p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="reading-meta" style="margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
      <span>⏱️ Estimated Reading Time: <strong>16 minutes (2,400+ words)</strong></span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- TABLE OF CONTENTS -->
  <section class="table-of-contents reveal" aria-label="Table of Contents">
    <span class="toc-kicker">Detailed Developer Navigation</span>
    <h2>Guide Contents</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#js-date-mechanics">1. The Native JavaScript Date Object Mechanics</a></li>
      <li><a href="#intl-datetimeformat">2. Formatting with Native Intl.DateTimeFormat</a></li>
      <li><a href="#temporal-api">3. The Future: The ECMAScript Temporal API</a></li>
      <li><a href="#libraries-comparison">4. Modern Libraries: Luxon, Day.js, date-fns-tz vs. Moment.js</a></li>
      <li><a href="#utc-database-serialization">5. UTC Storage &amp; ISO 8601 Serialization</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ssr-hydration-bugs">6. SSR Hydration Mismatches in Next.js &amp; Astro</a></li>
      <li><a href="#common-js-timezone-bugs">7. Common JavaScript Time Zone Gotchas &amp; Pitfalls</a></li>
      <li><a href="#code-snippets-cheatsheet">8. Code Snippets &amp; Execution Cheatsheet</a></li>
      <li><a href="#faq-section">9. Frequently Asked Questions (20 FAQs)</a></li>
      <li><a href="#js-tools">10. Developer Tools &amp; Converter Links</a></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 1: JS DATE MECHANICS -->
  <section id="js-date-mechanics" class="content-section reveal">
    <h2>1. The Native JavaScript Date Object Mechanics</h2>
    <p>
      Understanding time zones in JavaScript begins with recognizing what the legacy <code>Date</code> object actually is: an encapsulation of Unix Epoch milliseconds.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you execute <code>const d = new Date();</code>, JavaScript queries the system clock and stores a single integer (e.g., <code>1785420000000</code>). When calling methods like <code>d.getHours()</code> or <code>d.toString()</code>, the engine dynamically translates that UTC integer into the host environment's local time zone.
    </p>

    <!-- SVG DIAGRAM 1: JS Timezone Data Pipeline -->
    <div class="diagram-container" style="margin: 2.5rem 0; padding: 1.5rem; background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--text);">Figure 1: JavaScript Time Zone Execution Architecture</h3>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 800 280" width="100%" height="auto" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width: 760px; font-family: sans-serif;">
        <rect width="800" height="280" fill="var(--panel)" rx="8"/>
        
        <!-- Step 1: Input / Storage -->
        <rect x="40" y="80" width="200" height="120" rx="6" fill="var(--background)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="140" y="110" fill="var(--text)" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15">Database / API</text>
        <text x="140" y="135" fill="var(--muted)" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13">ISO 8601 UTC String</text>
        <text x="140" y="165" fill="var(--accent)" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">"2026-07-30T14:00:00Z"</text>

        <!-- Arrow 1 -->
        <line x1="240" y1="140" x2="300" y2="140" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="2.5"/>

        <!-- Step 2: JS Runtime Engine -->
        <rect x="300" y="80" width="200" height="120" rx="6" fill="var(--background)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="400" y="110" fill="var(--text)" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15">JS Date / Epoch</text>
        <text x="400" y="135" fill="var(--muted)" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13">Raw UTC Milliseconds</text>
        <text x="400" y="165" fill="#10b981" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">1785420000000</text>

        <!-- Arrow 2 -->
        <line x1="500" y1="140" x2="560" y2="140" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="2.5"/>

        <!-- Step 3: Render Layer -->
        <rect x="560" y="80" width="200" height="120" rx="6" fill="var(--background)" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="2"/>
        <text x="660" y="110" fill="var(--text)" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15">Client UI Render</text>
        <text x="660" y="135" fill="var(--muted)" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13">Intl.DateTimeFormat</text>
        <text x="660" y="165" fill="#3b82f6" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">"10:00 AM EDT"</text>

        <text x="400" y="245" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle">Rule: Transport as UTC -> Store as Epoch/ISO -> Format via Intl for Client Display</text>
      </svg>
      <p class="infographic-caption">The standard JS date flow: Store data in UTC, hold internally as Epoch milliseconds, and format into target IANA time zones at the UI boundary.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 2: INTL DATETIMEFORMAT -->
  <section id="intl-datetimeformat" class="content-section reveal">
    <h2>2. Formatting with Native Intl.DateTimeFormat</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code> API provides high-performance, native time zone formatting without third-party dependencies:
    </p>
    <pre style="background: var(--background); padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text);"><code>{`// Formatting a date into Eastern Time (EST/EDT)
const now = new Date();

const formatter = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', {
  timeZone: 'America/New_York',
  dateStyle: 'full',
  timeStyle: 'long'
});

console.log(formatter.format(now));
// Output: "Thursday, July 30, 2026 at 10:00:00 AM EDT"

// Detecting browser user timezone identifier
const userTimeZone = Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone;
console.log(userTimeZone); // e.g. "Europe/London" or "America/New_York"`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: TEMPORAL API -->
  <section id="temporal-api" class="content-section reveal">
    <h2>3. The Future: The ECMAScript Temporal API</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>Temporal</code> proposal solves legacy <code>Date</code> flaws by providing immutable, type-safe primitives:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>Temporal.Instant</code>: Represents a fixed point in time (UTC) without time zone ambiguity.</li>
      <li><code>Temporal.ZonedDateTime</code>: Represents a full wall-clock date and time in a specific IANA time zone (e.g., <code>America/New_York</code>).</li>
      <li><code>Temporal.PlainDateTime</code>: Represents a calendar date and clock time without an assigned time zone offset.</li>
    </ul>

    <pre style="background: var(--background); padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text);"><code>{`// Modern Temporal API Syntax
const zdt = Temporal.ZonedDateTime.from('2026-07-30T10:00:00-04:00[America/New_York]');
console.log(zdt.timeZoneId); // "America/New_York"

// Converting to GMT/London time seamlessly
const londonTime = zdt.withTimeZone('Europe/London');
console.log(londonTime.toString()); // "2026-07-30T15:00:00+01:00[Europe/London]"`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: LIBRARIES COMPARISON -->
  <section id="libraries-comparison" class="content-section reveal">
    <h2>4. Modern Libraries: Luxon, Day.js, date-fns-tz vs. Moment.js</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Library</th>
            <th>Bundle Size</th>
            <th>Immutability</th>
            <th>Time Zone Method</th>
            <th>Best Use Case</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Native Intl</strong></td>
            <td>0 KB (Built-in)</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td><code>timeZone: 'America/New_York'</code></td>
            <td>Modern browsers &amp; Node.js 18+ apps</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Luxon</strong></td>
            <td>~70 KB</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td><code>DateTime.now().setZone('America/New_York')</code></td>
            <td>Complex date math &amp; enterprise apps</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Day.js</strong></td>
            <td>~2 KB + plugin</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td><code>dayjs().tz('America/New_York')</code></td>
            <td>Lightweight React/Vue frontends</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>date-fns-tz</strong></td>
            <td>Tree-shakeable</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td><code>formatInTimeZone(date, tz, format)</code></td>
            <td>Functional programming codebases</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Moment.js (Legacy)</strong></td>
            <td>~300 KB+</td>
            <td>No (Mutable)</td>
            <td><code>moment.tz('America/New_York')</code></td>
            <td>Legacy maintenance ONLY</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5: UTC DATABASE SERIALIZATION -->
  <section id="utc-database-serialization" class="content-section reveal">
    <h2>5. UTC Storage &amp; ISO 8601 Serialization</h2>
    <p>
      Always convert dates to ISO 8601 UTC strings when transmitting via JSON APIs:
    </p>
    <pre style="background: var(--background); padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text);"><code>{`// Correct JSON payload serialization
const eventData = {
  title: "Global Tech Sync",
  startTime: new Date().toISOString() // "2026-07-30T14:00:00.000Z"
};
fetch('/api/events', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify(eventData)
});`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6: SSR HYDRATION BUGS -->
  <section id="ssr-hydration-bugs" class="content-section reveal">
    <h2>6. SSR Hydration Mismatches in Next.js &amp; Astro</h2>
    <p>
      In frameworks like Next.js, Nuxt, or Astro, rendering <code>new Date().toLocaleTimeString()</code> during Server-Side Rendering (SSR) causes hydration errors because the server renders UTC or server local time, while the browser hydration step renders user client time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution:</strong> Perform time zone formatting exclusively inside client-side lifecycle hooks (e.g., React <code>useEffect</code> or Astro <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags), or enforce a uniform time zone prop passed down from server to client.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 7: COMMON BUGS -->
  <section id="common-js-timezone-bugs" class="content-section reveal">
    <h2>7. Common JavaScript Time Zone Gotchas &amp; Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Date-Only String Parsing Trap:</strong> <code>new Date('2026-07-30')</code> parses as UTC midnight, causing off-by-one day errors in negative offsets. Solution: use explicit ISO timestamps <code>'2026-07-30T00:00:00'</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Inverted Sign in getTimezoneOffset():</strong> Remember that positive values mean <em>behind</em> UTC (New York = +300 minutes).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 8: CODE SNIPPETS -->
  <section id="code-snippets-cheatsheet" class="content-section reveal">
    <h2>8. Code Snippets &amp; Execution Cheatsheet</h2>
    <pre style="background: var(--background); padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text);"><code>{`// Helper: Convert GMT/UTC Date to EST/EDT String
function formatToEasternTime(dateInput) {
  return new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', {
    timeZone: 'America/New_York',
    year: 'numeric',
    month: '2-digit',
    day: '2-digit',
    hour: '2-digit',
    minute: '2-digit',
    second: '2-digit',
    hour12: true
  }).format(new Date(dateInput));
}

console.log(formatToEasternTime('2026-07-30T18:00:00Z'));
// Output: "07/30/2026, 02:00:00 PM"`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
  <section id="faq-section" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Frequently Asked Questions">
    <h2>9. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
    <p>Explore 20 detailed questions and answers about handling time zones in JavaScript:</p>

    <div class="faq">
      {meta.faqs.map((faq) => (
        <details style="margin-bottom: 1rem; padding: 1rem; background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 700; font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; cursor: pointer; color: var(--text);">{faq.q}</summary>
          <p style="margin: 12px 0 0; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.6;">
            <span set:html={faq.a} />
          </p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SUMMARY & RELATED RESOURCES -->
  <section id="js-tools" class="other-tools content-section" aria-label="Time Zone Links and Resources">
    <h2>10. Developer Tools &amp; Converter Links</h2>
    <p>
      Use our accurate developer time tools and converters for testing code:
    </p>
    <div class="tools-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
      <div class="tools-list">
        <h3>Primary Converters</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/">GMT to EST Converter</a></li>
          <li><a href="/est-to-gmt/">EST to GMT Converter</a></li>
          <li><a href="/gmt-vs-est/">GMT vs EST Comparison</a></li>
          <li><a href="/gmt-to-utc/">GMT to UTC Guide</a></li>
          <li><a href="/utc-vs-est/">UTC vs EST Guide</a></li>
          <li><a href="/gmt-to-pst/">GMT to PST Converter</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="tools-list">
        <h3>Developer Guides</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/time-zones-in-python/">Time Zones in Python Guide</a></li>
          <li><a href="/php-datetime-guide/">PHP DateTime &amp; Timezone Guide</a></li>
          <li><a href="/java-time-api/">Java Time API (JSR-310) Guide</a></li>
          <li><a href="/time-zone-database-iana/">IANA Time Zone Database Guide</a></li>
          <li><a href="/daylight-saving-time-explained/">Daylight Saving Time Explained</a></li>
          <li><a href="/military-time-converter-guide/">Military Time Guide</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="tools-list">
        <h3>Major City Clocks</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/new-york-time/">New York Time Clock</a></li>
          <li><a href="/london-time/">London Time Clock</a></li>
          <li><a href="/tokyo-time/">Tokyo Time Clock</a></li>
          <li><a href="/los-angeles-time/">Los Angeles Time Clock</a></li>
          <li><a href="/toronto-time/">Toronto Time Clock</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
